// Made by scripts/build-help.mjs from docs/guides and docs/runbooks. Don't edit this by hand.
export const HELP_CONTENT: Record<string, string> = {
  "admin-assessments": "# Admin: assessments and competencies\n\n## How it fits together\n\n- A **competency** is a skill or piece of equipment someone has shown they can handle.\n- A **serving role** lists the competencies it needs.\n- A **practical assessment** is how someone earns a competency.\n- **Approved to Serve** follows automatically once someone holds all of a role's competencies.\n\nThe observer records what they saw. The system decides the result.\n\n## Assessments\n\n**Waiting for assessment** lists volunteers who asked to be assessed. Choose **Assess now**. To record one nobody requested, use **Record an assessment**, and choose the practical and the person you watched. The list shows the department's members and anyone else who has started training there.\n\n1. Watch them work.\n2. Tick what you saw.\n3. Add feedback. It's worth writing, especially for a \"Not yet\".\n\nThe Academy works out the result from the leader's pass rule. You don't pick the outcome. A \"Not yet\" has no waiting period: they can ask again when they're ready.\n\n**Recent assessments** keeps each one with who observed it, what they saw, and the result. If one was recorded by mistake, **Withdraw this assessment**, with a reason. It stays on record, marked as withdrawn.\n\n## Competencies and roles\n\n**Competencies:** add the skills and equipment people need to show they can handle. Choose **Edit or archive** to change one. Each can have an expiry, if a skill needs refreshing.\n\n**Serving roles:** add the roles people serve in, such as \"Camera 2 operator\", then choose what each one needs.\n\n**People:** competencies appear here when someone passes a practical. If there's a reason to stop relying on one, choose **Suspend**, with a reason. You can restore it later.\n\n## Serving teams\n\nTeams are covered in [Scheduling](admin-scheduling.md). Passing an assessment never adds anyone to a team, and \"Trained\" or \"Still learning\" shows beside each name but doesn't gate anything.\n",
  "admin-checklists": "# Admin: checklists\n\nThese pages set up what each position does before and after a service. For how the team uses them, see [Checklist](checklist.md).\n\n## Task lists\n\nA **task list** is a full set of department checklists. New services use the **Default** list, and keep the list they started with. For a special service, make another list and choose it for that service. Make one as **An empty list**, or **A copy of** another.\n\nHiding a list keeps it under **Hidden lists**. You can't hide the default.\n\n## Making a checklist\n\n1. **Add a checklist** to a department. One per kind of position works well, such as Camera Operators.\n2. **Paste a list:** one item on each line. Start a line with `#` to begin a section. Bullets and numbers are removed for you. You can also add sections and items one at a time.\n3. **Who it's for:** choose the positions. People scheduled in these positions see this checklist first. Everyone serving can still see the rest.\n\n## Changing one\n\nUse **Edit, move or remove** on any section or item. If you remove a task, it disappears from current and future checklists. Past services keep their records, and you can bring it back from **Hidden items**.\n\n## Hidden items\n\nAnything you remove shows up here, newest first, with **Restore**. If what something belongs to is hidden too, restore that first.\n\n## Activity log\n\n**Activity log** shows every tick, untick, reset, and undo at a service, including attempts that were refused, and every change to the checklists. It can't be edited or deleted. Use it to work out what happened.\n\n## Service history\n\n**Service history** shows what was done and what was missed at each service. A service's record is fixed when its day ends, so later checklist changes never alter it.\n\n## Checklist settings\n\nFor church-wide checklist managers:\n\n- **Service type:** the checklist opens to the next service of this type. Other services still have checklists.\n- **Service day:** when nothing is published yet, the checklist still opens for this day so the team can tick. When Planning Center publishes the service, everything ticked stays with it.\n\n## If someone can't see their checklist\n\nUsually their Planning Center position isn't linked. The Task lists page warns you about it. Link it on the [Planning Center page](admin-scheduling.md).\n",
  "admin-curriculum": "# Admin: curriculum\n\n**Curriculum** is where you build courses, for a topic or for a level in a department. You publish your own department's work. Nobody else has to approve it, unless your department has turned on review before publishing.\n\n## Topics and departments\n\nThe row of buttons at the top starts with the **topics**, then a divider, then the **departments**.\n\n- A **department** is a team. Its courses sit in three levels, and they can have practical assessments.\n- A **topic**, such as General or Leadership, is courses for anyone on the production team. Some topics use levels and some are one list. Topics have lessons, quizzes, versions, and certificates, but no practical assessments, so the Practicals tab doesn't appear.\n\nProduction Directors build and publish topics. You add a topic under [Departments](admin-people.md#departments).\n\n## Course covers\n\nOpen a course's **Details** tab to upload its cover: upright, about 900 by 1200 pixels, as a PNG, JPEG, or WebP up to 2 MB. A cover isn't part of a version, so it shows straight away, with no draft to publish. Until a course has one, it shows its title on its category's colour.\n\n## Formatting text\n\nThe course summary, lesson summary, and lesson text work like a word processor. Use the buttons above the text for **Bold**, **Italic**, **Underline**, **Heading**, **Subheading**, lists, and **Link**. Links need to start with `https://`.\n\nIf you paste text that has `#` or `**` markers, they turn into real formatting. Volunteers see the same formatting on the course and lesson pages. Summaries can be up to 5,000 characters and lesson text up to 20,000, and the count under each box shows how much you've used.\n\n## The key idea: drafts and versions\n\nChanges stay in a draft until you publish, and volunteers keep seeing the live version until then. Each publish is kept as a version you can go back to. People's progress stays linked to the version they earned it on, so publishing never rewrites anyone's history.\n\n## Making a course\n\n1. Choose **Add course** under a level, and give it a title.\n2. Fill in **Course details:** a summary and objectives.\n3. Add a module, then add lessons to it.\n4. Add a quiz and a practical if the course needs them.\n5. **Publish,** with a change note saying what changed.\n\nTo change a published course, choose **Edit course**. That starts a new draft.\n\nIf you make a course by mistake and it's never been published, you can delete it from its row's menu. A course people have learned from can only be archived: volunteers stop seeing it, and its versions and everyone's history are kept.\n\n## Lessons\n\n| Field | Notes |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Title, module, summary** | What it is and where it sits |\n| **Objectives** | One per line. Shown as \"By the end of this lesson\" |\n| **Estimated minutes** | Optional |\n| **Video** | Optional. Either YouTube (unlisted) or the church video library. For YouTube, paste the link from the Share button |\n| **Resources** | Attach this department's SOPs. The lesson always shows each SOP's current version |\n| **Required to complete the course** | Leave it on unless the lesson is optional |\n\nLearners mark lessons complete themselves. The app never claims a video was fully watched.\n\n## Quizzes\n\nSet where the quiz sits, and its rules: the pass mark (80% to start with), how many attempts (3 to start with), whether correct answers are shown, whether questions shuffle, and whether it must be passed to finish the course.\n\nAdd questions with **Add question**. A quiz without questions can't be published. A question has a type, its answers, which one is correct, and an optional explanation shown with the correct answers when the quiz allows.\n\nThe app scores every attempt. Nobody marks them by hand. If someone runs out of attempts, open **Results** and choose **Give another attempt**, with a note such as \"Went through camera presets together\".\n\n## Practical assessments\n\nA practical is a checklist an observer ticks while watching someone work.\n\n1. **Add to checklist:** something they can watch happen, not a feeling, such as \"Frames the speaker with headroom\". Mark each item as required or not.\n2. **Settings:** where it sits, which competency a pass grants, and the pass rule. The usual rule is that every required item must be observed.\n\nThe observer ticks what they saw, and the Academy works out **Pass** or **Not yet** from your rule. See [Assessments and competencies](admin-assessments.md).\n",
  "admin-onboarding": "# Admin: onboarding\n\nOnboarding takes someone from \"I want to serve\" to ready for a team. You build each pathway's stages and steps. People move along by themselves, and you're told when they need you.\n\n## How people get in\n\n- **The sign-up page:** the public **Serve with us** page. Under **People in pathways**, choose **Share the sign-up page** to print it for the lobby or show it at a volunteer night, with a QR code.\n- **You add them:** **Add someone**, for someone you met in the lobby. They start at the pathway's first step.\n- **Joining a department** can start that department's pathway.\n\n## Building a pathway\n\nSet up **Join the team** first: everyone who wants to serve starts there, then moves on to a department's pathway.\n\nOpen **Onboarding pathways**. Add stages, such as \"Week 1\". Then drag steps from the **Step library** onto a stage, or press **Add**. Select a step to change what it does. A step marked **Needs setup** isn't finished, and **Waits here** means people pause on it until it's done.\n\nThe steps you can use:\n\n| Step | What it does |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Send an email** | Start from a template, then edit the subject and message. `{first name}` becomes their first name. **Emails are held until volunteer emails are switched on at launch, and emails held before then aren't sent later.** |\n| **Notify leaders** | Tells the leaders who look after the pathway |\n| **Follow-up task** | Something a leader has to do, with who reaches out and how many hours they have |\n| **Pair a mentor** | Sets up [mentoring](mentoring.md) |\n| **Assign a course** | Moves on by itself when they finish it |\n| **Wait some days** | Pauses before the next step |\n| **Choose a department** | The volunteer picks one on Training, under **Your next steps**. Leave departments unticked to offer every open one, including ones opened later |\n| **Add to a team in Planning Center** | A task for a leader, which completes itself when the sync sees them on the team. Planning Center stays read-only |\n| **Finish** | Ends the pathway |\n\nTo remove a stage, move or remove its steps first.\n\n## Follow-ups\n\n**Follow-ups** is what onboarding needs from you: **Yours**, **Anyone can take**, and **With other leaders**. Choose **I'll take this**, **Hand over** to someone else, or **Mark done**. Ticking one moves that person on to their next step.\n\n## People in pathways\n\n**People in pathways** shows who's where, how long they've been there, and what happens next. Open someone for their history and notes. A note can be shared with leaders who look after the pathway, or private to you.\n\nIf someone changes their mind, choose **Stop onboarding**. Their history and notes are kept, and you can add them again later.\n\n## What it doesn't do\n\nA pathway never adds anyone to a team and never approves anyone to serve. Courses only move a step on from real progress.\n",
  "admin-overview": "# Admin: start here\n\nIf you lead something, **Admin** is where you do it. Open your name, then **Admin**. The sidebar only shows what your role allows, and every page checks that again on the server. If a page says it isn't open to you, your role doesn't include it.\n\n**Back to Academy** is the purple button at the top of the sidebar. One section of the sidebar stays open at a time, and **Collapse all** closes it. A small dot on a closed section means you're in it, or something in it is waiting for you. The switch beside your name changes between light and dark.\n\n## The Dashboard\n\nThe first page answers two questions.\n\n- **Needs you:** things only you can move forward, such as requests waiting or assessments to do. Each one opens the page where you do it.\n- **Training, by department:** read each row left to right. People join, learn, get checked, then serve. A big drop between two numbers shows where people are getting stuck.\n\nBelow that is recent activity from the audit log.\n\n## Who can do what\n\n| Role | What it's for |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Volunteer** | Learns and serves. No admin abilities |\n| **Trainer or Evaluator** | Watches practical assessments for their department |\n| **Department Leader** | Runs one department: members, curriculum, SOPs, assessments, reports |\n| **Production Director** | The same across every department, plus choosing Service Dashboard editors |\n| **Scheduling Administrator** | Schedules and teams |\n| **Platform Administrator** | Accounts, roles, departments, and app settings, and drafting and publishing training and SOPs. It doesn't approve memberships |\n| **Service Dashboard Editor** | Changes the dashboard layout. Up to 3 people |\n\nSomeone can hold different roles in different departments, such as leading Audio while learning in Camera. You give out roles under [People and roles](admin-people.md).\n\n## How the app decides things\n\n- **The system decides results, not people.** Quizzes score themselves. A practical passes when the observer's checklist meets your pass rule, and the observer never picks the outcome. A pass grants the competency, and Approved to Serve follows automatically.\n- **Teams decide who serves, not training.** Training shows beside each name, and never blocks anyone.\n- **History is never rewritten.** People's progress stays linked to the version they earned it on.\n- **Sensitive changes are logged.** See the [audit log](admin-reports-mentoring-audit.md).\n\n## Where things are\n\n| Sidebar group | Pages | Guide |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Checklists** | Task lists, Activity log, Service history, Hidden items, Checklist settings | [Checklists](admin-checklists.md) |\n| **Services & Scheduling** | Planning Center, Serving teams, Schedule | [Scheduling](admin-scheduling.md) |\n| **Services & Scheduling** | Service Dashboard setup | [Service Dashboard](service-dashboard.md) |\n| **Training Academy** | Curriculum | [Curriculum](admin-curriculum.md) |\n| **Training Academy** | SOP library | [SOP library](admin-sops.md) |\n| **Training Academy** | Assessments, Competencies and roles | [Assessments and competencies](admin-assessments.md) |\n| **Training Academy** | Membership requests, Departments | [People and departments](admin-people.md) |\n| **Training Academy** | Mentoring, Reports | [Reports, mentoring, and the audit log](admin-reports-mentoring-audit.md) |\n| **People & Teams** | People and roles, Invitations | [People and departments](admin-people.md) |\n| **People & Teams** | Onboarding pathways, People in pathways, Follow-ups | [Onboarding](admin-onboarding.md) |\n| **System & Settings** | Audit log | [Reports, mentoring, and the audit log](admin-reports-mentoring-audit.md) |\n| **System & Settings** | App name and logo | [Service Dashboard](service-dashboard.md) |\n",
  "admin-people": "# Admin: people and departments\n\n## Membership requests\n\n**Membership requests** lists people asking to join your department, oldest first. Choose **Approve**. To add a note or decline, choose **More**. A note or a reason for declining is optional, and it's kept in the history.\n\nApproving makes someone part of the department's team. It doesn't approve them to serve. Courses are already open to anyone.\n\nThe **Members** view lists who's in each department. To take someone out, choose **Remove**. Their progress and history are kept.\n\n## Invitations\n\nUse **Invitations** to bring someone straight into a department. They join when they accept, because you've already decided.\n\n1. Choose **New invitation**.\n2. Pick a department, or **The Academy only, no department**.\n3. Say how many people can use it and how long the link works. A note for you is optional. Leave out personal details.\n4. Copy the link, or show its QR code on a screen or print it. **It's shown only once,** for security.\n\nTo stop a link, choose **Revoke**. It stops working straight away, and people who already joined stay members.\n\n## People and roles\n\n**People and roles** lists everyone who has signed in, 50 to a page. Open a person to:\n\n- **Grant a role,** choosing a department if the role needs one. Pick a role to see what it lets someone do.\n- **Remove a role,** with a reason. Past roles stay listed.\n- **Change their account:** Suspend, Deactivate, or Reactivate. Training history is kept whatever the status. On the People list, only accounts that aren't active have a label beside the name.\n\nYou can't change your own account status or remove your own roles, so one person can't lock everyone out by accident. Every change goes in the audit log.\n\nThe Service Dashboard Editor role is limited to 3 people, here as well as on the [dashboard's setup page](service-dashboard.md).\n\n## Departments\n\n**Departments** is for adding, opening, pausing, or renaming a department. Changes take effect straight away and go in the audit log. Nobody's training history changes.\n\n**Topics** have their own list lower on the page. A topic, such as General or Leadership, is courses only: nobody joins it, and it has no positions or practical assessments, so it doesn't appear in membership requests, invitations, teams, or reports. Choose **Add a topic** to add one, and tick **Use three levels** if its courses should be in levels.\n\nA new department gets the three levels straight away. For each one, you choose:\n\n| Setting | Choices |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Status** | Open now. Coming soon, which is seen but can't be joined. Hidden while you set it up |\n| **How people join** | They ask and a leader approves, anyone can join with no approval, or by invitation only. Joining never approves someone to serve. If you remove someone from a department anyone can join, they have to ask to come back |\n\nLighting Operations goes live just by changing its status here. No new release is needed.\n",
  "admin-reports-mentoring-audit": "# Admin: reports, mentoring, and the audit log\n\n## Reports\n\n**Reports** covers departments where you can view training reports. Choose a department and what to show:\n\n- **Course completion by volunteer:** who's where in each course, with certificates.\n- **Competencies and Approved to Serve by volunteer:** what each person holds.\n- **Approved to Serve by department and role:** how many people are ready for each role. Aim for at least two approved people in every role, so one absence doesn't leave a gap.\n\nReports include the department's members and anyone else who has started training there, because anyone can take any course. Starting a course doesn't make someone a member or put them on a team.\n\n**Download CSV** gives you the same data as a file. Downloads go in the audit log. They include names and training records only, and never mentoring's private notes.\n\n## Mentoring\n\n**Mentoring** pairs a volunteer with a trainer or leader. Choose the mentor and the volunteer, then **Pair them**. Pairing needs at least one trainer or leader and one member in the department.\n\nThey share goals and notes. You can read the shared ones. **A mentor's private notes stay private, even from you.**\n\nTo finish, choose **End this pairing**, with an optional reason. It stays on record for both of them. See [Mentoring](mentoring.md) for what they see.\n\n## Audit log\n\n**Audit log** lists every sensitive change, newest first: who did it, what happened, and when, in Winnipeg time. Entries can't be edited or deleted.\n\nUse it to answer \"who changed this, and when?\" It includes things like roles, memberships, publishing, overrides, report downloads, and Service Dashboard layouts and screens.\n",
  "admin-scheduling": "# Admin: scheduling\n\nPlanning Center Services schedules, and the Academy reads from it. The Academy never changes anything in Planning Center.\n\n## Planning Center\n\nThe **Planning Center** page is where the two connect.\n\n- **The top of the page:** the key numbers from the last read, which happens every 15 minutes. Choose **Refresh now** if you've just made a change and don't want to wait.\n- **Position links:** connect each Planning Center position to an Academy position, so people see the right checklist and their training. Your choice saves as soon as you pick it. On a phone, tap a row to choose. Planning Center gives each service type its own copy of a team, so link each one. Add Academy positions under [Competencies and roles](admin-assessments.md) first.\n- **Scheduled in positions not linked yet:** these people won't see their checklist for the service until you link their position.\n- **Not in the Academy yet:** people on a Planning Center team or schedule who haven't signed in. Nothing is sent to them. They get an account the first time they sign in with Planning Center.\n- **Who schedules:** Planning Center, or the Academy's own tools. Leave it on Planning Center unless you've decided to move away from it.\n\nThe setup is in the [Planning Center setup guide](../runbooks/scheduling.md).\n\n## Serving teams\n\n**Serving teams** shows who serves in each position, with their training beside their name, and what each position sees on the day. While Planning Center schedules, you add people to positions there, and they appear here after the next read.\n\nEach position has settings for the day:\n\n- **Sees all departments:** opens every department's checklist first.\n- **Leads the service:** can reset the checklists.\n- **Gets the Director role while scheduled.**\n\n## Schedule\n\n**Schedule** has two views while Planning Center schedules:\n\n- **Coming up:** each service, and how many positions are filled.\n- **Calendar:** every service this month. Tap a day to see who's serving and what's still open.\n\nTo change a service or who's on it, do it in Planning Center. It shows here within 15 minutes.\n\n## If the Academy schedules instead\n\nIf **Who schedules** is set to the Academy, you also get **Matrix** and **Service types**:\n\n1. **Service types:** set up each kind of service once, with its time and positions.\n2. **Make services:** create the next weeks in one step, repeating every 1 to 4 weeks. Dates that already have this service are skipped.\n3. **Matrix:** the next services side by side. Open a cell to schedule someone.\n\nA position is filled only from its team, never on a day someone is away, and never in a second position on the same day. **Override reason** lets you go past that if you must, and it's kept in the audit log. You can pair someone still learning with **Someone alongside**.\n",
  "admin-sops": "# Admin: SOP library\n\n**SOP library** holds the procedures for the departments you look after, and the ones for all of production.\n\n## Finding an SOP\n\nSOPs are grouped by category. Select a category's heading to collapse or open it, or use the category buttons to show one. You can also search, and filter by who it's for and its status. If any SOPs are due for review, a note at the top can show just those.\n\n## Adding an SOP\n\n1. Choose **Add SOP**. Give it a title, say who it's for, and choose a category or create a new one.\n2. Write the steps in the page, attach a file, or do both. A file can be a PDF, PNG, or JPEG up to 10 MB, and it's stored privately.\n3. Add a summary saying what it's for and when to use it. **Save draft** keeps your work.\n4. Choose **Publish** and say what changed. The draft becomes the current version.\n\nAn SOP needs written steps or a file before it can be published.\n\n## Writing the steps\n\nType in the page, and use the buttons above it to add headings, numbered steps, bullets, bold, and warnings. **Use a template** gives you a starting shape, and **Preview** shows what volunteers will see.\n\n## SOPs for all of production\n\nChoose **All of production** for a procedure that isn't about one department, such as how to do a copyright review. Everyone signed in can read the current version.\n\nOnly someone with organisation-wide rights, such as the Production Director or a Platform Administrator, can add or change these. Department leaders can attach them to their lessons.\n\n## Changing an SOP\n\nChoose **Edit SOP**. That starts a draft from the current version, and members keep seeing the current one until you publish. Say what changed. The previous version is kept and marked as replaced, and only the current version can be opened, so nobody follows an old procedure by mistake.\n\n## Keeping them current\n\nUnder **Settings**, set the owner, category, and how often it needs reviewing. When a review comes up, the owner is reminded.\n\nIf you've checked the current version and it's still right, choose to confirm the review. That doesn't need a new version.\n\n## Archiving\n\nChoose **Archive** at the top of an SOP's page when it's no longer in use. Members stop seeing it, and lessons stop listing it. Its versions and files are kept.\n\nArchived SOPs are hidden from the library until you set the status filter to **Archived**. Open one there, and **Delete** at the top removes it for good, with all its versions and files. It can't be undone, though the audit log keeps a note of what was deleted. If a live or draft course still lists the SOP, remove it from that lesson first.\n\n## Attaching to lessons\n\nAttach SOPs to a lesson under [Curriculum](admin-curriculum.md). The lesson always shows the current version, so you only update it in one place.\n",
  "checklist": "# Checklist\n\n**Checklist** is what each position does before and after a service. It opens to today's service, or the next one.\n\n## Using it\n\nYour own department's checklist comes first, marked **YOU**. Choose another department along the top to see theirs. Tick items as you go. The tick shows straight away and saves in the background. If a save fails, the tick goes back and tells you why.\n\nEveryone sees each other's ticks within about 30 seconds, without reloading.\n\n## When you can tick\n\nTicking opens two days before the service and closes twelve hours after it. Outside that, you can read it but not tick.\n\n## Who can see it\n\nThe Checklist is for the media team. Your access comes from your team in Planning Center. If you can't open it and think you should, ask a scheduling administrator to add you to a team.\n\n## If you're leading the service\n\nPositions that lead the service, such as Technical Director, can also:\n\n- **Reset checklists:** clears every tick for this service, for when it was ticked by mistake. The ticks are kept in the record.\n- **Undo the last reset:** brings those ticks back. Anything ticked again since stays as it is.\n\n## On the Service Dashboard\n\nThe [Service Dashboard](service-dashboard.md) has a Checklist tile. A tick there is the same tick as here, with the same rules. The wall TV only shows totals.\n\n## After the service\n\nEach service keeps its own record of what was done and what was missed. Every service starts fresh. Leaders can see past records under [Admin](admin-checklists.md).\n",
  "getting-started": "# Getting started\n\nThe Academy is where the production team at Immanuel Fellowship Church learns, gets ready, and sees what's happening on a Sunday. This page gets you signed in and shows you around.\n\n## Signing in\n\n- **If you're on a production team in Planning Center,** choose the Planning Center button. You don't need a separate password.\n- **If you're not,** choose **Not on a production team? Use an email link instead**, type your email, and open the link we send you. It works once. There's no password to remember.\n\n## Finding your way around\n\nThe side bar has these places. On a phone, the main four are along the bottom, and **All courses** is on Training.\n\n| Place | What it's for | Guide |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Training** | Departments, courses, and how far you've got | [Training](training.md) |\n| **All courses** | Every course, with search and filters | [Training](training.md) |\n| **Schedule** | When you're serving, and who with | [Schedule](schedule.md) |\n| **Checklist** | What to do before and after this service | [Checklist](checklist.md) |\n| **SOPs** | The team's written procedures | [SOPs](sops.md) |\n\nIf you're on a production team and using a computer, you also get the [Service Dashboard](service-dashboard.md).\n\nYour name, at the top right, opens a menu with the rest: your profile, notifications, getting the app, mentoring, help, and signing out. Leaders also see **Admin** there.\n\n## Three things that are kept separate\n\nPeople often assume these are one thing. They aren't.\n\n1. **Finishing a course** means you did the lessons and passed the quiz.\n2. **A competency** means someone watched you do the job and you met the checklist.\n3. **Approved to Serve** follows by itself once you hold every competency a role needs.\n\nWatching videos alone never approves you to serve. And being approved doesn't put you on the schedule: team managers decide who serves, and they can add you while you're still learning.\n\n## Your profile\n\nOpen your name, then **Your profile**. It shows the name leaders see, your departments and roles, and your certificates. If you signed in with Planning Center and your name differs there, you can use that one.\n\n## Notifications\n\nOpen your name, then **Notifications**. Everything shows here, such as being scheduled or an assessment result. Below that, you choose which kinds also come by email.\n\n## Put it on your phone\n\nOpen your name, then **Get the app**. It's free, takes a minute, and doesn't use an app store.\n\n- **iPhone or iPad:** open the Academy in Safari, tap **Share**, then **Add to Home Screen**, then **Add**.\n- **Android:** open the Academy in Chrome and tap **Install app**.\n- **Computer:** in Chrome or Edge, select the install icon at the right of the address bar.\n\n### Alerts\n\nOn the same page, choose **Turn on alerts** to get updates that drop down like any other app. You turn them on separately for each device. On iPhone and iPad, alerts need iOS 16.4 or later, and they only work once the Academy is on your Home Screen.\n\n## Dark or light\n\nUse the sun and moon switch beside your name to change between light and dark. It's remembered on each device.\n\n## Want to join the team?\n\nIf you're not serving yet, the **Serve with us** page is the place to start. Tell us your name and what interests you, and a leader will be in touch within a few days. No experience is needed.\n\n## Getting more help\n\nOpen your name, then **Help**, from any page. If you're on a screen with its own guide, that's where it opens. For anything else, ask your department leader or the Production Director.\n",
  "mentoring": "# Mentoring\n\nA department leader can pair you with a mentor: a trainer or leader who helps you grow. To find it, open your name, then **Mentoring**. If you'd like a mentor and don't have one, ask your department leader.\n\n## What's in a pairing\n\n| Part | Who can read it |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Goals** | Both of you and the department's leaders |\n| **Shared notes** | Both of you and the department's leaders |\n| **Your private notes** | Only you. They never appear in reports or exports |\n\nAdd a goal with an optional follow-up date. Add shared notes for anything you'd like to keep together, such as feedback after a service.\n\nWhen a pairing ends, it stays here, marked as ended, for reference.\n\nLeaders set up pairings under [Admin, Mentoring](admin-reports-mentoring-audit.md).\n",
  "schedule": "# Schedule\n\n**Schedule** shows when you're serving and who with.\n\n## Planning Center does the scheduling\n\nLeaders schedule in Planning Center, and the Academy reads from it every 15 minutes. So some things you do there, not here:\n\n- **Accept or decline** a request. Choose **Accept or decline in Planning Center**.\n- **Block out dates** you're away. Choose **Manage in Planning Center**.\n\nIf something you changed there doesn't show yet, wait up to 15 minutes. The page says when it last read from Planning Center.\n\n## What you'll see\n\n- **Today**, if you're serving today.\n- **Upcoming assignments:** each service, your position, and the time. Times are always Winnipeg time.\n- **Calendar:** your month at a glance, with a key to what each mark means. Tap a day to see it.\n- **Blocked dates** coming up.\n- **Who's serving with you** on each service. Choose it to see the team.\n- **Open checklist** takes you to that service's [Checklist](checklist.md).\n\n## Add it to your own calendar\n\nChoose **Add to your calendar (.ics)** to subscribe from your phone or computer's calendar app, so your assignments show up there too.\n\n## You aren't on the schedule yet?\n\nTeam managers add people to position teams in Planning Center when they're ready, and they schedule from those teams. Training shows beside your name as \"Trained\" or \"Still learning\", but it doesn't decide this. If you think you should be on a team, talk to your department leader.\n\n## If the Academy schedules instead\n\nAn administrator can switch scheduling from Planning Center to the Academy. If your church has done that, the page also offers **Days you're away**, **Ask for cover**, and **Open spots** to sign up for, and you accept or decline right here. It isn't switched on today.\n",
  "service-dashboard": "# The Service Dashboard: a guide for new people\n\n**Status:** being built in stages. This guide describes what was approved on 2026-11-02, and it gets updated every time the dashboard changes.\n\n**Working today:** the Service Dashboard page, the side bar link, one shared layout, editors, the layout editor, the app name and logo setting, and the tiles that come from Planning Center and the Academy: Run of service, Current item, Service timer, Who's serving, Checklist, Producer notes, and SOP shortcuts. The wall TV screen also works, and so does YouTube live once its setup is done (see the setup guide). To set up the connections, see the [Service Dashboard setup guide](../runbooks/service-dashboard.md). Slides, ProPresenter timers, Mixer levels, and Encoder health are built and show \"Not connected yet\" until the church bridge is set up. Items that have to be proven on real gear are listed at the end.\n\n## What it is\n\nThe Service Dashboard is one screen that shows the whole service while it runs: where we are in the plan, what's on the slides, and how the stream is doing.\n\nYou don't have to ask around or open six different apps. If you're serving, you can look up and see what's happening.\n\n**It only watches. It never controls anything.** Nothing you do on the dashboard can change a slide, move a fader, or stop the stream. You still run those from ProPresenter, the mixer, and the encoder, as you always have. If the dashboard stops working, the service carries on untouched.\n\n## Where to find it\n\n- **On a computer:** sign in to the Academy and choose **Service Dashboard** in the side bar.\n- **On the wall TV:** it's already there. The TV runs it by itself.\n- **On a phone or in the installed app:** it isn't available. A phone browser shows a message asking you to open it on a computer. Your training, schedule, checklist, and SOPs all still work on your phone.\n\nWho can open it follows Planning Center teams, the same way the Checklist does. If you're on a linked team, you have it. You don't need to finish any training first.\n\n## One layout for everyone\n\nService Producer, Audio, Graphics, Camera Director, and the wall TV all see the same screen. That means when someone says \"look at the run of service,\" everyone is looking at the same thing.\n\n## What each tile tells you\n\n| Tile                | What it shows                                                        | Where it comes from              |\n| ------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------- |\n| YouTube live        | **Live** or **Not live**                                             | YouTube                          |\n| Stream preview      | The YouTube live stream, a little behind the room. Off by default; an editor can add it | YouTube                          |\n| Current item        | What we're on, how long is left, and what's next                     | The plan and ProPresenter        |\n| Run of service      | Every item with its time, and the current one marked NOW             | Planning Center                  |\n| Slides              | The slide on screen and the next three                               | ProPresenter                     |\n| Service timer       | Time left in the whole service, and when it should end               | Worked out from the plan         |\n| ProPresenter timers | The timers running in ProPresenter                                   | ProPresenter                     |\n| Encoder health      | Whether the Magewell has a good input and is streaming                | Magewell Ultra Encode AIO        |\n| Who's serving       | Everyone on the team today, by position                              | Planning Center                  |\n| Checklist           | Today's tasks. Ticking one here is the same as ticking it in the app | The Academy Checklist            |\n| Producer notes      | Shared notes for this service                                        | Typed by the team                |\n| Mixer levels        | Signal levels for the channels the editors chose                     | The mixer                        |\n| SOP shortcuts       | Quick links to the procedures you need most                          | The Academy SOP library          |\n| Room loudness (SPL) | Switched off for now. See below                                      | A measurement mic, when we add it |\n\nEditors choose which tiles are on the layout, so you may not see all of these.\n\n### A few things that are easy to misread\n\n- **Current item goes by the plan's clock.** It works out where the service should be from the times in Planning Center, not what is really on screen. The Slides tile shows what is really on screen.\n- **The plan updates every 15 minutes,** so a late change in Planning Center can take that long to appear. The next two weeks of plans are read.\n\n- **The Slides tile may show words instead of pictures** if ProPresenter doesn't send a picture of a slide.\n- **Mixer levels are not room loudness.** They show the signal on each channel. The mixer can't measure how loud the room is. That takes a measurement mic or SMAART, which we don't have yet, so the Room loudness tile is built and left switched off.\n- **The stream preview runs behind the room.** That's normal for a livestream. Don't use it to judge timing.\n- **\"Trained\" and \"Still learning\" are information, not permission.** They show beside each name so you know who may need a hand. Team managers decide who serves.\n\n## What the messages mean\n\n| You see                     | What it means                                                                | What to do                                                       |\n| --------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Offline, last seen 10:12** | The tile lost its connection to the church gear. It shows the last reading   | Check that the bridge computer is on. The service isn't affected |\n| **Ready, switched off**     | The tile is built, but the gear it needs isn't connected                     | Nothing                                                          |\n\n## Changing the layout\n\nUp to three people can edit the layout. Everyone else can view it but can't change it.\n\nIf you're an editor, choose **Edit layout**. Then:\n\n- **Add a tile:** drag it in from the list.\n- **Move a tile:** drag it by its dots.\n- **Resize a tile:** drag its bottom-right corner.\n- **Remove a tile:** choose **Remove**.\n- **On a keyboard:** focus a tile, use the arrow keys to move it, Shift and the arrow keys to resize it, and Delete to remove it.\n\nChoose **Save layout** when you're done. Every computer and the wall TV pick up the change.\n\nRemember that the wall TV has to fit everything on one screen with no scrolling. The more tiles you add, the smaller the text gets, so check the TV after a big change.\n\n### Choosing mixer channels\n\nOn the Mixer levels tile, choose **Choose channels**, or go to **Service Dashboard setup**, then **Mixer channels**. Tick any channels, aux inputs, FX returns, mix buses, matrix outputs, or mains, use **Up** and **Down** to set the order, and choose **Save channels**. Until someone chooses, the tile shows the mains and the first eight channels. This only changes what the dashboard shows. It never touches the mixer.\n\n## The wall TV\n\nThe TV has no computer, mouse, or keyboard, and nobody signs in on it.\n\nOur TV is likely a Roku TV, and Roku has no proper web browser. So a small Android TV or Google TV stick plugs into the HDMI port and runs the dashboard full screen.\n\nThe TV is always in the black theme. On a computer, the dashboard follows the light and dark switch beside your name.\n\nOn the TV, checklists show totals, such as \"5 of 8\", and notes can be read but not edited.\n\n**To set up a TV, one time:**\n\n1. Plug in the stick and open the screen address, which is the Academy's address followed by `/screen`. The TV shows a 6-digit code.\n2. On your phone or computer, sign in and go to **Admin**, then **Service Dashboard setup**, then **Screens**. Enter the code.\n3. On the Roku TV, go to **Settings**, then **System**, **Power**, and **Power on**, and choose that HDMI input. The menu can differ by TV brand. This sends the TV back to the dashboard after a power cut.\n\nThe TV holds a view-only pass, not anyone's account. It can't tick checklists or edit notes. To disconnect a TV, choose **Remove** beside it in **Screens**, and it's signed out straight away.\n\n## How it gets its information\n\n**From the internet, with nothing to do each week:**\n\n- **Planning Center** provides the plan, the times, and who's serving. The Academy only reads from Planning Center and never changes it.\n- **YouTube** only needs our channel and an API key, because our stream is public. Nobody signs in. The Magewell goes live as usual, and the YouTube live tile says so. It checks from 30 minutes before the service until 30 minutes after, while a screen is open, so keep the wall TV or a booth computer on. It can take a couple of minutes to change to **Live**.\n\nThe dashboard doesn't count viewers. To see how many people are watching, check YouTube Studio or Facebook directly.\n\n**From the church network, through the bridge:**\n\nThe cloud can't reach the mixer, ProPresenter, or the Magewell directly. So a small program called the **bridge** runs on a church computer. It reads those three and sends the readings out to the Academy about once a second. It only connects outward, so no ports are opened on the church network.\n\nIf that computer is off, the tiles that depend on it go faded and say **Offline**. Everything else keeps working.\n\n## After the service\n\nChoose **After the service** at the top of the dashboard. It builds itself from what was recorded: how long each item ran against the plan. Use it to see where the service ran long.\n\n- **Actual times** come from Planning Center, and appear only when someone runs the plan with Services LIVE. Without that, an item says \"Not recorded\".\n\n## App name and logo\n\nPlatform administrators can change the app's name, short name, and logo under **Admin**, then **App name and logo**.\n\n- **New installs** get the change straight away.\n- **Android** picks it up by itself, but it can take days, and the name may not change in every case.\n- **iPhone** will most likely need the app removed and added again.\n- **Certificates already issued** keep the name they were issued under.\n- **The sign-in email and the offline page** keep the original name for now, because they are fixed files.\n\n## Words we use\n\n| Word          | Meaning                                                                       |\n| ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Tile          | One box on the dashboard                                                      |\n| Layout        | Which tiles appear and where                                                  |\n| Editor        | One of up to three people allowed to change the layout                        |\n| Bridge        | The small program on a church computer that reads the gear                    |\n| Screen        | A TV connected by code, with a view-only pass                                 |\n| Scribble strip | The small name display above each channel on the mixer                       |\n| SPL           | Sound pressure level: how loud the room actually is                           |\n\n## Still to be proven\n\nThese were approved knowing they need a real test. This guide will change once they're done.\n\n- **ProPresenter:** the exact way to read slide status, and whether it can send pictures of the next three slides. If it only sends text, those tiles show the words.\n- **X32:** the exact meter format, and reading channel names from the scribble strips.\n- **Magewell:** whether its web API needs a sign-in.\n- **YouTube:** how soon after going live the YouTube live tile changes, on our real channel.\n- **The TV stick:** that it reopens the dashboard by itself after a power cut.\n- **Services LIVE times:** that they arrive as expected, the first time someone runs a plan live.\n- **Text size on the TV:** whether it can be read from where people stand.\n- **If the mixer changes:** the Allen & Heath dLive has a documented connection, but its meter access is unconfirmed. We found no documented way to read meters on the Waves LV1 Classic, so that tile would likely go away.\n",
  "sops": "# SOPs\n\nSOPs are the team's written procedures: how to start the stream, how to set up a camera, what to do when something goes wrong.\n\n## Finding one\n\nOpen **SOPs** in the side bar and start typing to search. Results update as you type, and search looks in titles, summaries, and categories. You can also choose a category, see what was **Recently updated**, or narrow it by department. Some SOPs are for **All of production** rather than one department.\n\nThe list shows when each SOP was last reviewed, and marks any that are due for review. Open an SOP to see its summary and owner.\n\n## Reading one\n\nOpen an SOP to read its steps, and open its file if it has one, usually a PDF. Some have both, such as written steps and a wiring diagram.\n\n- **Only the current version can be opened,** so nobody follows an old procedure by mistake. Version history shows what changed and when.\n- **\"Due for review\"** means nobody has confirmed it's still right lately. It's probably fine, but check with its owner if something looks off.\n- **\"Archived\"** means it's no longer in use. It's kept for the record.\n\n## Where else you'll see them\n\nLessons can attach SOPs, and always show the current version. The [Service Dashboard](service-dashboard.md) can also have shortcuts to them.\n\nLeaders look after these under [Admin, SOP library](admin-sops.md).\n",
  "training": "# Training\n\n**Training** is your home in the Academy. It shows what to do next, your departments, and rows of courses by topic and department. **All courses** lists every course, with search and filters for category and level. On a phone, you'll find it under **Courses** on Training.\n\n## Anyone can learn anything\n\nEvery open department's courses are open to you. You don't need anyone's permission to start one, and you can take a course simply because you're curious.\n\n**Joining a department is a separate thing.** It means you're part of that department's team, so its leader knows you and can plan around you. To join, choose **Request to join** on a department. A leader reviews each request, and some departments are by invitation only.\n\n## Topics\n\n**Topics**, such as General and Leadership, are courses for everyone on the production team, whichever department you serve in. There's nothing to join: open a topic and start a course. Finishing one earns a certificate, but it doesn't count towards being Approved to Serve.\n\n## How a department is set out\n\nEach department has three levels, one step at a time. Each level has courses, and each course has lessons, and usually a quiz and a practical assessment.\n\n| Label on a department | What it means |\n| --- | --- |\n| **See courses** | It's open. Go ahead. |\n| **Coming soon** | You can see what's planned, but training opens when the curriculum is ready. |\n| **Join** | Anyone can join this department straight away. |\n| **By invitation** | Ask the department leader for an invite to join. |\n| **Request sent** | A department leader will review it. |\n\n## Taking a course\n\nOpen a course and choose **Start the course**, or **Continue** to pick up where you left off.\n\n### Lessons\n\nA lesson may have a video, what you'll learn, and attached SOPs. When you've done it, mark it **Completed** yourself. The Academy never claims a video was fully watched, so it's on your honour.\n\nSome lessons are optional. The course outline marks them. Only the ones needed **To finish the course** count.\n\n### Quizzes\n\nThe app scores your answers against the pass mark the department set. That's usually 80%, with 3 attempts, though a department can change either.\n\n- If you don't pass, go back over the lessons and try again.\n- If you've used all your attempts, talk to your trainer or leader. They can give you another one.\n- You can leave and come back: an unfinished attempt waits for you under **Resume the quiz**.\n\nPassing a quiz counts towards the course. It doesn't approve you to serve.\n\n### Practical assessments\n\nThis is where someone watches you do the job.\n\n1. Open the practical, and read **What the trainer watches for**.\n2. When you're ready, choose **Request assessment**. A trainer will arrange a time with you.\n3. They tick what they saw on the checklist. You don't get a score from them: the app works out the result from the department's pass rule.\n\nThe result is **Pass** or **Not yet**. \"Not yet\" has no penalty and no waiting period. Practise, and ask again when you're ready.\n\nA pass grants the competency. If that was the last one a role needs, you're **Approved to Serve** in that role automatically.\n\n## Certificates\n\nWhen you finish a course, its certificate appears on the course and on your profile, ready to download. Each one has a reference and a QR code, so anyone can check it's real. If a certificate was issued by mistake, a leader can withdraw it. It then says so, and the record is kept.\n\n## Your next steps\n\nIf you're being welcomed onto the team through an onboarding pathway, **Your next steps** shows at the top of Training: where you are, who your contact is, and what's next. Some steps move on by themselves when you finish a course.\n\nFinishing courses never decides on its own whether you serve. A team leader adds you to a team when you're ready.\n\n## Lesson content changes\n\nLeaders update courses over time. What you've already earned stays linked to the version you earned it on, so an update never takes away your progress or certificate.\n",
  "runbook-backups": "# Backups, the restore test, and the weekly summary\n\nSupabase Free keeps no backups, so the Academy makes its own (SPEC §9 \"Backup design\"):\n\n- **Weekly** (`.github/workflows/backup.yml`, Sundays 08:00 UTC; weekly since 2026-11-01): the whole database (`pg_dump`\n  custom format, version 17 like Supabase) and every stored SOP file, encrypted with `age` on\n  the runner, uploaded with SHA-256 checksums to the **IFC Academy Backups** Shared Drive. Old\n  copies are deleted: the last 30 are kept (about 7 months).\n- **Monthly** (`restore-test.yml`, the 3rd, 10:00 UTC): downloads the newest backup, checks its\n  checksums, decrypts it, restores it into a throwaway Postgres 17 container that uses\n  Supabase's own image, and compares row counts with the counts taken when it was made.\n- **Weekly** (`weekly-summary.yml`, Mondays 14:00 UTC): emails when each job last succeeded and\n  commits `status/last-run.json`, which stops GitHub disabling scheduled jobs after 60 quiet days.\n  A keep-alive with no success for two days, or a backup with none for eight, counts as an incident\n  and alerts the admins.\n\nNone of it logs data: only status, timings, sizes, and file counts. Nothing is ever uploaded as a\nworkflow artifact. Backups and restore tests are **off until the repository variable\n`BACKUPS_ENABLED` is `true`**; until then scheduled runs show as skipped.\n\n## One-time setup\n\n### 1. The encryption keys\n\nOn a trusted computer, install [age](https://github.com/FiloSottile/age) and make two keys:\n\n```sh\nage-keygen -o academy-backup.key        # the real key: keep it offline\nage-keygen -o academy-restore-test.key  # only for the monthly restore test\n```\n\n- **`academy-backup.key`** decrypts every backup. Store it offline (for example a password\n  manager entry or printed in a safe) with the Production Director and the Platform Admin. Never\n  put it in Google Drive or GitHub.\n- **`academy-restore-test.key`** lets the monthly job prove a backup restores. It lives in GitHub\n  secrets, so anyone who can change this repository's workflows could use it to decrypt a backup\n  they also downloaded from the Shared Drive. If that trade-off isn't acceptable, leave it out:\n  backups still run, and you restore-test by hand each month with the offline key instead.\n\nEach key file prints its public key (`age1…`). Both public keys go into `AGE_RECIPIENTS`.\n\n### 2. The Shared Drive, service account, and keyless sign-in\n\nNew Google Cloud organizations block service-account key files\n(`iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation`), and keyless is safer anyway. The jobs use **Workload\nIdentity Federation**: GitHub proves to Google that it's this repository's job on `main`, and\nGoogle returns a short-lived Drive token. There's no key to leak.\n\n1. In IFC's Google Workspace, create a **Shared Drive** named \"IFC Academy Backups\". Limit members\n   to the Production Director and the Platform Admin. Its folder ID is the last part of its address.\n2. In Google Cloud, in the backup project, enable the **Google Drive API**, **IAM Service Account\n   Credentials API**, and **Security Token Service API**.\n3. Create a **service account** (`academy-backup`), with no roles and no keys.\n4. **IAM & Admin → Workload Identity Federation → Create pool**: name and ID `github`. Add a\n   provider:\n   - **OpenID Connect (OIDC)**, name and ID `github-actions`\n   - Issuer URL `https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com`, audiences: default\n   - Attribute mapping: `google.subject` = `assertion.sub`, `attribute.repository` =\n     `assertion.repository`, `attribute.ref` = `assertion.ref`\n   - Attribute condition, so only this repository's `main` gets in:\n     `assertion.repository == \"gboyegaadeojo/IFC-Production-Academy\" && assertion.ref == \"refs/heads/main\"`\n5. On the pool, **Grant access → Grant access using service account impersonation**: choose\n   `academy-backup`, attribute `repository`, value `gboyegaadeojo/IFC-Production-Academy`. Skip\n   the configuration file download.\n6. Add the service account's email to the Shared Drive as a **Content manager** (no notification).\n7. Note the provider's resource name,\n   `projects/<project number>/locations/global/workloadIdentityPools/github/providers/github-actions`.\n\n### 3. The database connection string\n\nIn Supabase → Connect → **Session pooler** (IPv4), copy the connection string and put in the\ndatabase password. Use the session pooler: GitHub's runners can't reach Supabase's direct\n(IPv6) address. The same secret lets every deploy apply new database migrations before the app\ngoes live; without it, deploys warn that migrations weren't applied.\n\n### 4. Secrets and the variable\n\n```sh\ngh secret set SUPABASE_DB_URL               # the connection string from step 3\ngh secret set SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY     # Supabase → Project Settings → API keys\ngh secret set AGE_RECIPIENTS                # both public keys, separated by a space\ngh secret set AGE_RESTORE_IDENTITY < academy-restore-test.key   # optional, see step 1\ngh secret set GDRIVE_FOLDER_ID\ngh variable set GCP_WORKLOAD_IDENTITY_PROVIDER --body \"projects/<number>/locations/global/workloadIdentityPools/github/providers/github-actions\"\ngh variable set GCP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT --body \"academy-backup@<project>.iam.gserviceaccount.com\"\ngh variable set BACKUPS_ENABLED --body true\n```\n\nThe two `GCP_` values are identifiers, not secrets: without this repository's `main` branch they\nopen nothing. (Where key files are allowed, a `GDRIVE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON` secret still works\ninstead.)\n\n`SUPABASE_URL`, `RESEND_API_KEY`, `ALERT_EMAILS`, and `ALERT_FROM` are shared with the other jobs.\nThen delete `academy-restore-test.key` from the computer.\n\n### 5. Check it\n\nRun **Backup** by hand (Actions → Backup → Run workflow). The log should show sizes and\n\"Retention: … kept, 0 deleted\", and three files should appear in the Shared Drive. Then run\n**Restore test** by hand and look for \"Restore test: pass\".\n\n## Restoring for real\n\nTarget: back online within 4 hours, losing at most a week of data.\n\n1. Download the newest `academy-db-….dump.age`, `academy-files-….zip.age`, and\n   `academy-….sha256` from the Shared Drive, and check them: `sha256sum -c academy-….sha256`.\n2. Decrypt with the offline key:\n   `age -d -i academy-backup.key -o db.dump academy-db-….dump.age` (and the same for the files).\n3. Create a fresh Supabase project (or reset the existing one), then restore:\n   `pg_restore --no-owner --no-privileges -d \"<session pooler connection string>\" db.dump`.\n   Supabase's own schemas already exist, so \"already exists\" messages are expected.\n4. Re-upload the files: unzip `files.zip`; each top-level folder is a Storage bucket. Upload them\n   to the same paths (for example with the Supabase CLI `supabase storage cp -r`).\n5. Point the app at the project: update `SUPABASE_URL` and `SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY` in GitHub\n   and run the deploy. Check you can sign in and that a course and an SOP open.\n6. Delete every decrypted copy.\n",
  "runbook-monitoring": "# Monitoring\n\nSentry tells you when the app breaks. PostHog counts which pages get used. Both are free tiers, and\nboth are off until you set their keys below. While they're off, the browser doesn't download either\none.\n\n## Turn them on\n\nAdd these under GitHub → Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions → **Variables**, then run the\nDeploy workflow. They're variables because both values reach the browser, so they can't be secret.\n\n| Variable | Where it comes from |\n| --- | --- |\n| `SENTRY_DSN` | Sentry → create a Next.js project → Project Settings → Client Keys (DSN) |\n| `POSTHOG_KEY` | PostHog → Project Settings → Project API key |\n| `POSTHOG_HOST` | Only for PostHog's EU region: `https://eu.i.posthog.com`. Leave it out for the US region |\n\nIn PostHog, also turn on **Discard client IP data** under Project Settings.\n\n## What gets sent\n\n**Sentry** receives the error, where in the code it happened, the browser, and the page's address.\nStack traces from the browser are minified, because source maps aren't uploaded.\n\n**PostHog** receives anonymous page views, counted per device, and no other kind of event. It\ndoesn't record sessions, clicks, or page speed, and it isn't told who anyone is, so it can't show\none person's activity. Training numbers such as\ncompletion rates come from the Academy's own reports.\n\n## What never gets sent\n\n`lib/domain/monitoring.ts` removes these before anything leaves the app:\n\n- names, email addresses, cookies, request headers, and request bodies\n- query strings, which can hold live room tickets and sign-in codes\n- invite tokens and certificate references in addresses\n- IDs, which become `[id]`\n- quoted values in error text, which is where database errors put names\n- page titles, which can name a person or course\n\nAddresses are scrubbed at any depth, because both tools repeat them in fields of their own.\n\nIf you add a page whose address holds a secret, add it to `PRIVATE_PATHS` in that file. After any\nchange to the monitoring code or a library upgrade, repeat the check below with a fake invite link,\nsuch as `/invite/fake-token?code=fake`, and search what's sent for those values. PostHog ignores\nautomated browsers, so an automated test needs to hide that it's one.\n\n## Check it works\n\n1. **Sentry:** after deploying, open the app, then run\n   `setTimeout(() => { throw new Error(\"Monitoring test\"); })` in the browser's console. It should\n   appear in Sentry within a minute.\n2. **PostHog:** open a few pages, then look under Activity. Open an event and check that its address\n   has no query string or ID.\n",
  "runbook-nas-media": "# Church video library (UniFi NAS)\n\nLessons can play training videos stored on the church's UniFi NAS, as well as unlisted YouTube\nvideos (decided 2026-10-30). This page is for whoever sets it up at IFC. It contains no hostnames,\naddresses, or credentials, and none should ever be added to this repository.\n\n## How it works\n\n```\nVolunteer's browser\n  → the Academy (Cloudflare Worker): checks they're signed in and may read the lesson\n  → Cloudflare Tunnel hostname, protected by Cloudflare Access (service token only)\n  → cloudflared and a read-only file server on a small computer at church\n  → the NAS training-videos share, mounted read-only\n```\n\n- The browser only ever sees `/media/lessons/<lesson id>` on the Academy. It never sees the NAS,\n  the tunnel hostname, or a file path. The link can't be shared: it plays only for someone signed\n  in who may read that lesson, which makes it suitable for restricted material (SRS-SOP-006).\n- Every request is checked, including each part of the video the player asks for while seeking.\n- Until it's set up, leaders can still add library file paths to lessons. Learners see \"The church\n  video library isn't connected yet\".\n\n## What you need\n\n- **A small always-on computer on the church network**, such as a Raspberry Pi 4 or 5, an old\n  mini PC, or any Docker host. The UniFi NAS may not be able to run the tunnel itself.\n- **A NAS account just for this**, with read-only access to one share or folder that holds only\n  the exported training videos. Don't share the whole NAS.\n- The church's **Cloudflare account** (the one that hosts the Academy). Tunnel and Access are on\n  the free plan (Access is free for up to 50 users; this uses one service token).\n\n## Setup\n\n1. **Export the videos for the web.** Use MP4 with H.264 video and AAC audio, about 720p at 2–3\n   Mbit/s, and turn on \"fast start\" (web optimised) so playback begins before the whole file\n   arrives. WebM also works. Use simple file names: letters, numbers, spaces, `.`, `_`, `-`.\n   Organise by department and level, for example `camera/level-1/framing.mp4`.\n2. **Mount the share read-only** on the small computer (SMB or NFS), for example at\n   `/srv/training-videos`.\n3. **Run a read-only file server on localhost only**, such as Caddy:\n\n   ```\n   :8080 {\n     bind 127.0.0.1\n     root * /srv/training-videos\n     file_server        # no \"browse\": directory listings stay off\n   }\n   ```\n\n4. **Create a Cloudflare Tunnel** (Zero Trust → Networks → Tunnels) and install `cloudflared` on\n   the computer as a service. Add a public hostname on the church's domain that points to\n   `http://127.0.0.1:8080`.\n5. **Protect the hostname with Cloudflare Access** (Zero Trust → Access → Applications →\n   Self-hosted). Add one policy, with action **Service Auth**, that allows only a new service token\n   named for the Academy. Don't add any email or \"everyone\" rules. Copy the token's client ID and\n   secret once; Cloudflare doesn't show the secret again.\n6. **Give the Academy the three secrets** (Workers → the Academy → Settings → Variables and\n   Secrets, as type Secret, or `wrangler secret put`):\n   - `MEDIA_ORIGIN_URL`: `https://` plus the tunnel hostname.\n   - `MEDIA_ACCESS_CLIENT_ID` and `MEDIA_ACCESS_CLIENT_SECRET`: from step 5.\n\n   Don't put them in GitHub, an issue, a chat, or this repository.\n7. **Check it.**\n   - Add a library file to a test lesson, publish it, and play it as a member, seeking back and\n     forward.\n   - Then open the same lesson address while signed out, and as someone outside the department:\n     both must fail.\n   - Opening the tunnel hostname directly in a browser must show the Access block page.\n\n## Things to know\n\n- **Bandwidth.** Every viewer streams from the church's internet connection, at about 2.5 Mbit/s\n  per viewer. The church has a 1 Gbit/s upload (confirmed 2026-10-31), so the line is not the\n  limit: the small computer is. Use wired Ethernet (gigabit), not Wi-Fi, between it, the NAS, and\n  the router. If the connection changes, keep popular, non-sensitive videos on unlisted YouTube.\n- **Downtime.** If the small computer, the NAS, or the church internet is down, library videos\n  show \"The church video library isn't reachable right now\" and the lesson text still works.\n- **Cloudflare's terms.** Check Cloudflare's current terms for serving video through Workers and\n  Tunnel before go-live. Training volume is small, but this needs confirming, not assuming.\n- **Rotating the token.** Create a new service token, add it to the Access policy, update the two\n  secrets, then delete the old token.\n- **Logs** record only status codes and timings, never file paths, people, or tokens.\n",
  "runbook-notifications": "# Notification emails\n\nThe Academy writes a notification when something happens for a person: joining a department,\nbeing scheduled, a practical assessment result, a mentoring pairing, or an SOP they own coming\ndue for review. Everyone sees theirs in the app under **Notifications** (account menu), and\nchooses which kinds also come by email.\n\nEmails are sent every 15 minutes by `.github/workflows/notifications.yml`, which runs\n`scripts/send-notifications.ts`. A Cloudflare timer starts it, because GitHub's own schedule can run\nhours late ([how it works](scheduling.md#starting-jobs-on-time)). Until the secrets below are set,\nnothing is emailed and notifications stay in the app.\n\n## Secrets (GitHub → Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions)\n\n| Secret | What it is |\n| --- | --- |\n| `SUPABASE_URL` | Already set for the keep-alive job |\n| `SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY` | Supabase → Project Settings → API keys → service role. Only ever an Actions secret: never in the app's runtime, the browser, the repo, or a log |\n| `RESEND_API_KEY` | Already set for failure alerts |\n| `NOTIFY_FROM` | The sender, for example `IFC Production Academy <academy@your-domain>`, on a domain verified in Resend |\n\nThe Academy's address for links in emails comes from the `SITE_URL` repository variable the\ndeploy workflow already uses.\n\n## What the job does\n\n1. Queues reminders (`queue_sop_review_reminders` and `queue_reminders`), each once:\n   - SOP review due a week before the review date, and overdue a week after it;\n   - \"Please confirm\" 48 hours after someone is scheduled, if they haven't answered;\n   - \"Serving soon\" from 10:00 two days before an event, to the confirmed volunteer and their\n     supervisor;\n   - \"Keep going\" 7 and 21 days after someone last worked on a course they started;\n   - a practical assessment request still open after 7 days, to the department's leaders;\n   - a competency expiring within 30 days, where expiry is on.\n\n   An assessment request also notifies the department's trainers the moment it's made.\n   Times follow decision 8 in `docs/decisions/2026-10-31-open-decisions.md`.\n2. Holds email during quiet hours, 20:00 to 08:00 Winnipeg time; it goes out at 08:00. Sign-in\n   links aren't affected, because Supabase sends them.\n3. Claims up to 50 pending emails at a time (`claim_email_notifications`), skipping kinds the\n   person turned off and accounts that aren't active.\n4. Sends each through Resend and records the result (`complete_email_notification`). A failed\n   send is retried on the next runs, up to three attempts.\n\n5. Sends onboarding pathway emails the same way (`claim_onboarding_emails`,\n   `complete_onboarding_email`), if onboarding emails are on. See below.\n\nThe job logs counts only: no addresses, names, or content. If every send in a run fails, the job\nfails and the failure alert emails the admins.\n\n## Onboarding emails\n\nThese go to people on a pathway, who may not have an account yet. They're off until launch: while\n`organizations.onboarding_emails_enabled` is false, each email is written to `onboarding_outbox`\nas `held` and nothing is sent.\n\nTo turn them on, at launch only, set `organizations.onboarding_emails_enabled` to true. From then\non, new emails are written as `pending` and go out on the next run, outside quiet hours.\n\n- **Emails held before that stay held.** They aren't sent when you switch on, so nobody gets a\n  welcome written for them weeks ago. Look through them and contact anyone who still needs one.\n- **Someone whose account is suspended or deactivated isn't emailed.** Their email is marked\n  `skipped`.\n- **People without an account are told to reply** if they'd rather not get these, so someone must\n  read the `NOTIFY_FROM` inbox.\n\n## Check it works\n\n- Run the workflow by hand (Actions → Notifications → Run workflow) and look for\n  `Notification emails: N sent, 0 failed`.\n- Schedule a test volunteer (an address you control), wait for the next run, and check the email\n  arrives with a link back to the Academy.\n\n## Resend's limits\n\nThe free plan allows 100 emails a day and 3,000 a month, shared with sign-in links, and 2 requests a second. The sender waits between emails. When Resend says it's at its limit, the rest stay in the queue and go out on a later run, without using up a try. Any other failure gets 3 tries, and the job fails loudly so you're told.\n",
  "runbook-planning-center-sign-in": "# Sign in with Planning Center\n\nThe production team signs in with their Planning Center account (checklist app US-01). Planning\nCenter governs sign-in; the email link stays under \"Use an email link instead\" for people who only\nwant training. A sign-in without Planning Center that has people choose their departments is\nplanned as the very last piece of work.\n\n## Who gets in\n\n- People in IFC's Planning Center as the sync sees it: on a production team's roster or on a\n  schedule. Someone just added in Planning Center can sign in after the next read (15 minutes, or\n  **Refresh now**).\n- Only from IFC's Planning Center organization. The sync records its id.\n- The first time, the Academy creates their account quietly (no email) with their Planning Center\n  name and primary email, and links it to their Planning Center person. If an account already uses\n  one of their Planning Center emails, that account is linked instead. The link is permanent:\n  changing their email in Planning Center doesn't matter.\n- Suspended or deactivated accounts still can't use the Academy.\n\n## Setup (done 2026-10-31)\n\n1. In Planning Center: **api.planningcenteronline.com/oauth/applications → New application**.\n   Redirect URI, exactly:\n\n   ```text\n   https://ifc-production-academy.media-5f7.workers.dev/auth/planning-center/callback\n   ```\n\n2. Cloudflare secrets (paste each value when asked; never in a chat or a file):\n\n   ```sh\n   npx wrangler secret put PCO_CLIENT_ID\n   npx wrangler secret put PCO_CLIENT_SECRET\n   npx wrangler secret put SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY\n   ```\n\nUntil all three are set, the sign-in page shows the email link only.\n\n## How it works\n\n`/auth/planning-center` sends the person to Planning Center with a one-time state stored in an\nhttpOnly cookie. The callback checks the state, swaps the code for a token on the server, reads\n`/people/v2/me` (with emails) and the organization, and calls `planning_center_sign_in()` with the\nservice role. The token is used once and never stored or logged. The server then starts an\nordinary Academy session.\n",
  "runbook-push-alerts": "# Device alerts (web push)\n\nAlerts that drop down on phones and computers, like a native app's, for anyone who turns them on under **Notifications → Alerts on this device**. Decided by G on 2026-11-01: the Academy is installed as a Home Screen app (no app stores), and alerts arrive within seconds. Details are in `docs/decisions/2026-11-01-app-and-alerts.md`.\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Something writes a notification (`private.notify`).\n2. A database trigger calls `POST /api/push/dispatch` through `pg_net`, sending the notification id and a token that the database generated itself (`private.app_settings`). The token never appears in the repo or a log.\n3. The app claims the notification once (`claim_push`), gets the person's devices, and sends each one an encrypted alert (`lib/domain/web-push.ts`, RFC 8291 and RFC 8292).\n4. Anything not sent within a minute is picked up by `notifications.yml` (every 15 minutes) through `claim_push_batch`. Alerts older than a day are dropped.\n5. A device the push service reports as gone (404 or 410) is deleted.\n\nQuiet hours don't apply to alerts, because phones have their own Do Not Disturb. Emails still respect quiet hours.\n\n## Setup (once)\n\n1. Run `npm run push:keys` from the repo, signed in to the GitHub CLI. It makes a VAPID key pair (P-256) and saves:\n   - GitHub variable `VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY`: the public key (base64url, 65 bytes uncompressed).\n   - GitHub secret `VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY`: the private key (base64url `d`, 32 bytes). It's never printed or written to disk.\n2. Deploy (push to `main`, or run the Deploy workflow):\n   - The build gets the public key.\n   - The deploy copies the private key to the Worker with `wrangler secret put`.\n   - The deploy points the database at `<SITE_URL>/api/push/dispatch`.\n\nUntil both keys are set, alerts stay off and the Notifications page says so.\n\n## Rotating the keys\n\nNew keys invalidate every device's subscription. Rotate only if the private key leaks. After rotating (`npm run push:keys` again), everyone must turn alerts on again on each device.\n\n## iPhone and iPad\n\nAlerts work only after the Academy is added to the Home Screen and opened from there, on iOS or iPadOS 16.4 or later. The Notifications page explains this and shows the Share → Add to Home Screen steps.\n\n## Checking\n\n- Notifications page → **Send a test alert**: it goes straight to that device, with no notification written.\n- The `notifications.yml` log shows counts only: `Device alerts: N sent, N devices gone, N failed`.\n",
  "runbook-scheduling": "# Scheduling: Planning Center and the Academy\n\nPlanning Center Services schedules production. The Academy reads it every 15 minutes and shows it\nto volunteers with their checklists and training (decision record 9). Schedulers and volunteers\nkeep using Planning Center as they do now.\n\n## 1. Connect Planning Center (once)\n\nThe Academy uses the same read-only Personal Access Token as the Sunday Pre-Service Checklist app.\nIt needs **Services** and **People** (People is used only to read email addresses for matching).\n\nIn a terminal, in this repository, run each line and paste the value when asked. Never paste a\nsecret into a chat, an issue, or a file:\n\n```sh\ngh secret set PCO_PAT_ID\ngh secret set PCO_PAT_SECRET\n```\n\nThen run the job once by hand: **Actions → Planning Center sync → Run workflow**. Its log shows\ncounts only, such as \"plans 9, scheduled 31, people matched 18\". If it says Planning Center refused\nthe token, the token is wrong or doesn't include Services and People.\n\n### Starting jobs on time\n\nGitHub's own schedule can run hours late, so a Cloudflare timer on the Worker asks GitHub to run\nthe Planning Center sync and the notifications job every 15 minutes. The jobs and their secrets stay\non GitHub. GitHub's schedule stays on as a backup, so if the timer stops, the jobs still run, later.\n\n**Refresh now** on the Planning Center page runs the sync straight away instead\nof waiting up to 15 minutes. The button and the timer use the same token, which can only ask GitHub\nto run jobs. The Planning Center token stays in Actions secrets.\n\nTo set up that GitHub token:\n\n1. On GitHub: **Settings → Developer settings → Fine-grained personal access tokens → Generate\n   new token**. Resource owner: you. Repository access: **Only select repositories**, this one.\n   Permissions: **Actions: Read and write**, nothing else. Pick an expiry and note it.\n2. Add it to Cloudflare as a secret (paste when asked; never in a chat or file):\n\n   ```sh\n   npx wrangler secret put GITHUB_DISPATCH_TOKEN\n   ```\n\nUntil it's set, the button doesn't show and the timer starts nothing. The button can be pressed once\nevery two minutes, and each press is in the audit log.\n\n**When the token expires,** the button says it couldn't reach GitHub, Sentry shows \"Could not start\nscheduled jobs\", and the jobs fall back to GitHub's slower schedule. Make a new token and put it\nagain. To check the timer is working, look under **Actions** for runs of these jobs marked\n`workflow_dispatch`, about every 15 minutes.\n\n## 2. Link positions\n\nIn the Academy, open **Admin → Planning Center**. After the first read it lists each Planning\nCenter service type, team, and position, like the checklist app's Team mapping.\n\n1. Create matching Academy positions first, in **Competencies and roles** (for example Camera 1,\n   Camera 2, Audio, ProPresenter, Production Director, Technical Director, Miscellaneous), each in\n   its department.\n2. On the Planning Center page, link each Planning Center position to its Academy position. A team\n   can also be linked as a whole; positions without their own link follow it.\n3. Links apply at the next read, within 15 minutes, or straight away with **Refresh now**.\n\n**Scheduled in positions not linked yet**, on the Position links tab, lists people on an upcoming service whose Planning\nCenter position isn't linked, or whose position name matches nothing in Planning Center. They\nwon't see a checklist until it's linked. Pre-service checklists shows the same warning for the\ncurrent service.\n\nThe **Not in the Academy** tab lists people on a Planning Center team or schedule with no Academy\naccount using one of their Planning Center emails. Invite them with that email.\n\n## 3. Set what each position sees on the day\n\nIn **Serving teams**, each position shows who is on it (from Planning Center's roster) and two\nswitches, as in the checklist app:\n\n- **Sees all departments:** whoever is scheduled here sees every checklist by default\n  (Technical Director).\n- **Gets the Director role while scheduled:** can reset and undo the service's checklists\n  (Technical Director, and Production Director if you like).\n\n## 4. Bring in the checklists\n\nIn **Pre-service checklists**, add one checklist per kind of position (Camera Operators, Audio\nEngineer, Presentation / Computer Graphics, Director (Switcher), Technical Director,\nMiscellaneous), each in its department, and link it to the positions that use it. Use **Paste a\nlist** to bring in the current checklist without retyping it: ready-to-paste text for all six\nlists is in the `private/checklists/` folder on G's computer, kept out of the public repository.\n\nThen, under **Pre-service checklists → Settings**, choose the Planning Center **service type** the\nchecklist follows (such as the Sunday service), so a youth night or rehearsal earlier in the week\ndoesn't open first, and the usual **service day** (Sunday). When nothing is published yet, the\nchecklist still opens for that day with a note saying so; once Planning Center publishes the\nservice, the next sync gives it that day's checklist, ticks included.\n\n## What volunteers see\n\n- **Your schedule:** where they're serving (from Planning Center), a month calendar, who's serving\n  with them, and their blockout dates. Accept, decline, and block out dates in Planning Center;\n  changes show within 15 minutes.\n- **Checklist:** today's service, or the next (or the next service day, if nothing is published\n  yet). Scheduled volunteers see their position's\n  checklist first; production team members who aren't serving can still help.\n\n## If IFC ever schedules in the Academy\n\nThe Academy's own scheduler (service types, the Matrix, sign-ups, and swaps) is built but switched\noff. An organisation-wide scheduler can switch it on under **Who schedules** on the Planning Center\npage. While Planning Center schedules, the Academy refuses schedule changes from anyone but the\nsync, so the two can't drift apart.\n\n## Service Dashboard run of service\n\nThe same job reads each plan's items, songs, item notes, and Services LIVE times from yesterday through the next 14 days, and applies them with `sync_service_plan_items()`. That's one extra request per plan, and it needs no new token or permission. It's read-only, like the rest. Past services keep the run of service they had.\n",
  "runbook-service-dashboard": "# Service Dashboard: setup\n\nFor whoever sets up the connections. New people should read [the guide](../guides/service-dashboard.md) instead.\n\nThe dashboard only reads. Nothing here can change a slide, the mixer, the encoder, or the stream.\n\n## YouTube live status and preview\n\nThe stream is public, so nobody signs in and nothing expires.\n\n1. **Channel ID:** in the Academy, go to Admin, Service Dashboard setup, Connections, and paste the channel ID. It starts with `UC`. The stream preview works from this alone.\n2. **API key, for the YouTube live tile:**\n   1. In [Google Cloud Console](https://console.cloud.google.com/), create a project, or use the backups project.\n   2. Enable **YouTube Data API v3**.\n   3. Under Credentials, create an **API key**. Restrict it to YouTube Data API v3.\n   4. Add it as a Cloudflare secret named `YOUTUBE_API_KEY` (Workers, the Academy, Settings, Variables and Secrets, as type Secret, or `wrangler secret put YOUTUBE_API_KEY`).\n\n**How it stays free:** it only looks from 30 minutes before a service until 30 minutes after. It searches for the stream every 2 minutes at most until it finds it, then checks every 30 seconds at most, however many screens are open. Google allows about 100 searches a day, and each check of a found stream costs far less.\n\n**Still to test on the real channel:** how soon after going live the search finds the stream.\n\nThe checks run while a screen is open during the service, so leave the wall TV on or a booth computer open.\n\n## Wall TV\n\n1. **Plug a small Android TV or Google TV stick into the TV's HDMI port.** A Roku TV can't run this by itself, because Roku has no proper web browser.\n2. Install a full-screen browser app on the stick, and open `<the Academy's address>/screen`. The TV shows a 6-digit code.\n3. On a phone or computer, sign in, go to Admin, Service Dashboard setup, Screens, and enter the code. This is for the editors and for whoever chooses them.\n4. Set the app to open that address when the stick starts.\n5. **On a Roku TV:** go to Settings, System, Power, Power on, and choose that HDMI input, so the TV returns to the dashboard after a power cut. The menu can differ by TV brand.\n\nThe TV holds a long-lived, view-only pass in a cookie, stored in the database only as a hash. To disconnect a TV, choose Remove beside it in Screens, and its pass stops working on its next refresh, within 30 seconds. If a TV's browser data is cleared, it shows a new code and needs adding again.\n\n**Still to test on the real stick:** that it reopens by itself after a power cut, and that the text can be read from where people stand.\n\n## The church bridge\n\nThe cloud can't reach the mixer, ProPresenter, or the Magewell, so a small program on a church computer reads them and sends the readings out. It only connects outward, so no ports open and no firewall changes are needed. It only reads: it never sends anything that can change a slide, the desk, or the encoder. It logs status and counts, never lyrics or names.\n\n**1. Make a secret, and give it to the Academy.** Make a long random secret (for example `openssl rand -hex 32`). Add it as a Cloudflare secret named `LIVE_ROOM_SECRET` (or `wrangler secret put LIVE_ROOM_SECRET`). Keep a copy for step 3. Without it the live tiles say \"Not connected yet\", and nothing else changes.\n\n**2. Put the program on a church computer** that stays on during services and is on the same network as the gear. It needs [Node.js 24](https://nodejs.org) and this repository, with no install step:\n\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/gboyegaadeojo/IFC-Production-Academy.git\ncd IFC-Production-Academy\n```\n\n**3. Try it with example readings first,** which needs no gear:\n\n```bash\nACADEMY_URL=https://<the Academy's address> LIVE_ROOM_SECRET=<the secret> node bridge/main.ts --simulate\n```\n\nNode may print a warning about the module type. That is harmless.\n\nThe Slides, Timers, Levels, and Encoder tiles should fill with obviously fake readings. Stop it with Ctrl+C. A tile fades and says **Offline** about 10 seconds later.\n\n**4. Point it at the real gear.** Leave out any you don't have, and that tile says it's not reachable:\n\n| Setting | What it is |\n| --- | --- |\n| `X32_HOST` | The X32's IP address (it uses UDP port 10023) |\n| `PROPRESENTER_URL` | `http://<its computer>:<port>`, with Network turned on in ProPresenter's preferences, where the port is shown |\n| `MAGEWELL_URL` | `http://<the encoder's address>` |\n| `MAGEWELL_USER`, `MAGEWELL_PASSWORD` | The encoder's sign-in, if it asks for one |\n\n**5. Start it with the computer,** using Task Scheduler on Windows or a systemd service on Linux, with those settings as environment variables. Don't put the secret in a file inside this repository.\n\n**To update it:** `git pull`, then restart it.\n\n**Free tier:** one message a second for a 4-hour Sunday counts as about 720 requests of the Durable Object's 100,000 a day, and about 1,800 of its 13,000 GB-s. Sending to screens is free. Slide pictures only go when the slide changes.\n\n### Still to test on the real gear\n\nThe bridge is built from each maker's documentation and tested with those documents' own examples, but it hasn't met the real gear yet. Check these first, using each tile:\n\n- **X32:** the levels move, and channel names appear. Each of the desk's meter sets is documented, but note that it allows only a few clients at once.\n- **ProPresenter:** the slide on screen and the next three are right, and pictures appear. Its published API and a client library disagree on one address, so the bridge tries both.\n- **Magewell:** whether it needs a sign-in, and that streams show correctly. Its `is-use` and `result` fields are what the bridge reads for \"Streaming\".\n\n### Another mixer\n\nLevels come from `bridge/x32.ts`. The Allen & Heath dLive has a documented connection, though its meter access is unconfirmed. We found no documented way to read meters on the Waves LV1 Classic. Another desk needs its own reader reporting the same channel ids (`lib/domain/mixer.ts`). Until then, leave `X32_HOST` out, and the other tiles carry on.\n"
};
